  <h1>Boiler Broken on a Sunday or Bank Holiday? Here's When We're Actually Answering the Phone</h1>
  <p>Boilers have a habit of picking the worst moment to give up. Saturday night, Christmas Day, the one bank holiday Monday you had plans for. Before you start ringing round, here's the honest answer to what actually happens when you contact us outside normal hours, rather than a vague "we're always here for you" that falls apart the second you try it.</p>

  <h2>What happens if you call outside office hours</h2>
  <p>Our phone lines are staffed {siteConfig.officeHours}. Call outside that window and you won't hit a dead-end answerphone — you'll get Tom. Worth being straight about what that actually is: Tom's a voice AI we built to cover that gap properly, not one of us and not a call centre. He'll take down exactly what's wrong with your <a href="/boiler-repairs-breakdowns/">boiler breakdown</a> and your details, and flag it if it sounds urgent — no heating, no hot water, a leak — so it's sitting ready and triaged rather than buried in a pile of voicemails by the time the office opens. He can also answer the simple stuff on the spot: our hours, roughly what a job tends to cost, whether we cover your area.</p>
  <p>We built Tom instead of the two usual options: an answerphone that goes nowhere until morning, or a 24-hour call centre staffed by people who've never heard of York, let alone your street, with the overhead quietly added to your invoice somewhere down the line. A two-person outfit, Steve and Kyle, can give you a fixed £120 call-out and someone who actually knows the area, or it can promise a round-the-clock switchboard and charge you for it later. We've kept the first one — Tom's just there so you're not left with silence in between.</p>

  <h2>Why WhatsApp is the better move late at night</h2>
  <p>This is the bit most people don't expect: message us on WhatsApp at <a href={`https://wa.me/${siteConfig.whatsappNumberIntl}`}>{siteConfig.whatsappDisplay}</a> any time, and it gets picked up first thing, before the phone lines even open. Tell us what the boiler's doing, any error code showing, and whether it's heating, hot water, or both. By the time we're at our desks, you're already at the front of that morning's queue instead of starting from scratch with a 9am phone call alongside everyone else who's just woken up to the same problem. For an emergency plumber York families can actually reach out of hours, that message is the quickest way in. Ringing and talking to Tom works just as well if typing it out isn't convenient — either route lands in the same place and gets looked at the same way first thing.</p>

  <h2>How we decide who gets to first, once the day starts</h2>
  <p>When the phones open, not every call can be first. It roughly sorts itself like this:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>No heating or no hot water at all, especially where there's an elderly relative, a baby, or someone unwell in the house</li>
    <li>A leak that's actively getting worse or anywhere near electrics</li>
    <li>No hot water on its own, which is unpleasant but usually less urgent than a <a href="/advice-centre/no-heating-no-hot-water/">no heating, no hot water</a> situation in the middle of winter</li>
    <li>A boiler that's making a noise or playing up but still keeping the house warm, which can usually wait for the next slot</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If someone vulnerable is affected, say so when you message. It won't change what the job costs, but it does change where you sit on that list.</p>

  <h2>The nights this actually matters most</h2>
  <p>Winter is when a boiler not working York call stops being an inconvenience and starts being properly uncomfortable. January is reliably the busiest month we see, and the whole run from December through February brings far more breakdowns than any other stretch of the year. If your boiler's getting on a bit, that's exactly when it tends to pick to fail, usually on a Friday night or over a long weekend when it feels like nobody's about.</p>

  <h2>So what should you actually do tonight?</h2>
  <p>If it's gas you can smell, that's not a boiler job at all. Stop, get out, and call the National Gas Emergency line on 0800 111 999. If it's a standard emergency boiler repair situation, no heat, no hot water, a leak, a strange noise, call us and tell Tom what's happening, or send a WhatsApp message instead, whichever's easier right now. Keep the heating off if there's any doubt about safety, close the door on the room if it's leaking near electrics, and layer up rather than sit shivering waiting for a reply that won't land until morning anyway.</p>
  <p>We'll have your message waiting when the office opens and give you a straight answer on timing then, rather than a promise at midnight we can't actually keep. Call <a href={siteConfig.phoneHref}>{siteConfig.phoneDisplay}</a> during the day, or message <a href={`https://wa.me/${siteConfig.whatsappNumberIntl}`}>{siteConfig.whatsappDisplay}</a> any time in between.</p>
